-- examples/checks/asks-demand.rofl — A RELATION ANSWERED ON DEMAND IS WHAT SOME RULE CALLED.
-- dd_u has a variable no premise binds, so a fact is made of it for each call. dd_u is asked and no asked
-- relation reads it: its rows are the calls of dd_h, a rule outside any cone it makes. The world without asks
-- holds dd_u(3, 3); a cone that kept dd_h out would hold none, so asking a relation answered on demand keeps
-- every rule.
asks(dd_u).
edb(dd_q).
dd_q(3).
dd_u(X, Y) :- dd_q(X).
dd_h(X) :- dd_q(X), dd_u(X, X).
-- expect-row: dd_u[main](3,3)
-- expect-row: dd_h[main](3)
